---
import LandingLayout from '../../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/landing/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/landing/Footer.astro';
import { BOOKMARKLET_FALLBACK_ORIGIN, buildBookmarkletHref } from '../../scripts/bookmarklet';

const title = 'Try the agentlet bookmarklet';
const description = 'A bookmarklet that loads the agentlet live demo on agentlet.io from any of its pages.';
---

<LandingLayout title={title} description={description}>
	<Header />
	<main id="main">
		<section>
			<div class="wrap bookmarklet-page">
				<h1>Try the agentlet bookmarklet</h1>
				<p>
					Drag the button below to your bookmarks bar, or grab the "agentlet demo" chip next to the
					button in the <a href="/">home page</a>'s hero. Clicking either loads the same live demo as
					that page's "Try it on this page" button, on whichever agentlet.io page you are currently
					viewing.
				</p>
				<p>
					<a
						class="button button-primary bookmarklet-link"
						id="bookmarklet-link"
						href={buildBookmarkletHref(BOOKMARKLET_FALLBACK_ORIGIN)}
					>
						Agentlet demo
					</a>
				</p>
				<p class="bookmarklet-hint">
					Most browsers show the bookmarks bar with a keyboard shortcut such as Ctrl+Shift+B
					(Windows and Linux) or Cmd+Shift+B (macOS). Drag the button above onto it, then click it on
					any page here.
				</p>

				<p>
					Looking for demos that run on other sites, such as Wikipedia? They use a separate
					bookmarklet, on <a href="/try/known-sites/">the known-sites page</a>.
				</p>

				<h2>What it does</h2>
				<p>
					The bookmarklet is a small piece of JavaScript, stored as the link's own address instead of
					an ordinary URL. Clicking it runs that code on the page you are currently on: it loads
					agentlet-core and the demo registry from this site, the same way the hero button does, and
					opens the panel.
				</p>

				<h2>Where it works</h2>
				<p>
					It only works on agentlet.io. A page's Content Security Policy decides whether a
					bookmarklet can run any code at all, and whether that code can then load further scripts.
					agentlet.io's policy, site-wide, allows inline scripts and same-origin scripts, which is what
					this bookmarklet needs. Most other sites set a stricter policy and block bookmarklets
					entirely, which is expected: this is a way to try the demo here, not a general-purpose
					deployment method. See{' '}
					<a href="/docs/concepts/deployment-modes/">deployment modes</a> for how a real bookmarklet deployment
					differs.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</LandingLayout>

<style>
	.bookmarklet-page {
		max-width: 42rem;
		padding-block: 1rem 3rem;
	}

	.bookmarklet-page h1 {
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}

	.bookmarklet-page h2 {
		margin-top: 2rem;
	}

	.bookmarklet-page p {
		margin: 1rem 0;
	}

	.bookmarklet-link {
		cursor: grab;
	}

	.bookmarklet-hint {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
	}
</style>

<script>
	import { BOOKMARKLET_FALLBACK_ORIGIN, buildBookmarkletHref } from '../../scripts/bookmarklet';

	const link = document.getElementById('bookmarklet-link');
	if (link) {
		const origin = window.location.origin || BOOKMARKLET_FALLBACK_ORIGIN;
		link.setAttribute('href', buildBookmarkletHref(origin));
	}
</script>
